Apply the case evidence: This matches the textbook emphasis on operating cash flow. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. Combined annual depreciation for the three assets is €32,858.

The underlying topic is income statement, cash flow statement, and financial statement timing. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

Apply the case evidence: Sum of annual charges ≈ €35,417. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. After three years, the delivery truck''s carrying value is €33,574.

The underlying topic is income statement, cash flow statement, and financial statement timing. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

Apply the case evidence: Carrying value ≈ €29,500. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Residual value of the €52,000 delivery truck is ignored when calculating its annual depreciation.

The underlying topic is income statement, cash flow statement, and financial statement timing. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

Apply the case evidence: Residual value is deducted from cost before spreading the remainder. Check whether the direction of change (higher/lower, above/below the threshold) matches what the statement asserts.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. After three years, the computer equipment, originally costing €20,000, is fully written down to nil.

The underlying topic is income statement, cash flow statement, and financial statement timing. When figures appear, the exam checks whether you can interpret them in accounting terms—what increased, what ratio is implied, and whether the label (current, non-current, equity, and so on) fits.

