No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. A profit earned during the year increases the equity reported on the balance sheet, usually because it is added to retained earnings.

This is an accounting reasoning item on income statement, cash flow statement, and financial statement timing. Decide whether the sentence correctly describes how a transaction, balance-sheet line, or period result affects assets, liabilities, or equity.

From the figures or classification rule involved, profit raises the owners'' stake in the business by boosting retained earnings within equity. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.', 'TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. A loss incurred during the year reduces the equity reported on the balance sheet.

This is an accounting reasoning item on income statement, cash flow statement, and financial statement timing. Decide whether the sentence correctly describes how a transaction, balance-sheet line, or period result affects assets, liabilities, or equity.

From the figures or classification rule involved, a loss lowers retained earnings and therefore reduces total equity. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. Profit for the year and the net change in cash and cash equivalents for the year are different measures that will not usually be equal.

This is an accounting reasoning item on income statement, cash flow statement, and financial statement timing. Decide whether the sentence correctly describes how a transaction, balance-sheet line, or period result affects assets, liabilities, or equity.

From the figures or classification rule involved, non-cash items and timing differences mean profit and cash movement typically diverge. Accounting statements fail when a loss is treated as increasing equity, when a flow is placed on the balance sheet, or when a ratio inequality is reversed.

